# A credential must never be committed. See README.md.
keys.local.json
__pycache__/
cache/

# The NLS layer catalogues. Not committed, and the reason is a licence and not size:
# the National Library of Scotland asks that these georeferenced layers be re-used
# "within a desktop or local environment" and that a public site use their Historic
# Maps API instead. This repository is intended to become public, and a ready-made
# catalogue of the layers they ask not to be served publicly is the wrong thing to
# publish even though it is only URLs. Rebuild with tools/nls_catalogue.py.
nls_*.json

# The first-edition compositor and its county index. Local only, and for the same
# reason as the catalogues above rather than because of the Historic Counties data in
# counties.json, which is freely usable: composite.js line 24 holds the base URL of the
# NLS England-and-Wales six-inch bucket, and counties.json enumerates the 53 slugs that
# complete it. Together they are an index — plus a working fetcher — to layers whose
# addresses the Library does not itself publish and asks not to be served publicly.
# Separately harmless; published together they are the thing the catalogues were
# gitignored to avoid. Found by rewt-86.
composite.js
counties.json

# The local viewer's own backdrop list. Same reason again: 67 filled-in bucket URLs.
#
# THE LINE THAT DECIDES IS THE HOST, NOT WHETHER THE LAYER IS HISTORIC, and this comment
# said the opposite until 3 September. It read "the published viewer's
# docs/viewer/backdrops.json ... offers OpenStreetMap and nothing else", which was true
# when it was written and is not now: that file has twelve options — OpenStreetMap,
# three Carto basemaps, and SEVEN historic NLS layers delivered through MapTiler
# (mt_six_inch_1888, mt_one_inch_1885, mt_osgb_1900s, mt_osgb_1919, mt_prov_1937,
# mt_seventh_1955, mt_london_1893). Nothing is wrong with the file; MapTiler is the
# route the Library's own sentence points at, "please use our Historic Maps API layers".
# What was wrong was this note, which is the reasoning somebody reads before deciding
# whether a new layer may go in the published viewer — and as written it said no
# historic mapping may, so the next reader would either believe something false about
# the file or conclude the rule had been quietly broken.
#
# The rule, stated so it cannot go stale the same way: a published backdrop may not
# serve tiles from mapseries-tilesets.s3.amazonaws.com. That is the whole of it. The
# sanctioned route is fine and is used. `_removed_nls` in docs/viewer/backdrops.json is
# the record of the decision, and tests/test_licence.py now holds its first half — the
# comment is no longer the only thing standing between the rule and a rebuild. Found by
# rewt-c1; the file was measured before this was rewritten.
backdrops.json
